{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Upon the face of the field.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): since tzara'at is an adhesive disease, therefore he must send it away to a place without settlement, so that the tzara'at not spread. And this is the reason for the impurity of the metzora: because of the spirit of impurity that has alighted upon him, from the bands of the forces of impurity that emanate from the primordial serpent, the father of fathers of impurity, who spoke evil of his Creator — and this metzora is punished because he walked in his path and spoke evil and resembled him; therefore Scripture likens the metzora to a corpse, which is the father of fathers of impurity. <br>Further, <b>upon the face of the field</b> alludes to the denizens of the field, for the live bird, dipped in the blood of the slaughtered bird, he would send to the denizens of the field — and they are the band of destroyers below, of lower rank than the prince of the wilderness who accepts bribe on the Day of Atonement, and it bears the iniquities of the metzora in the manner of the goat sent to Azazel in the wilderness. And he was not permitted to send it neither to the wilderness nor to the sea, and this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded in Torat Kohanim: 'upon the face of the field' — that he must not stand in Jaffa and send it to the sea, nor stand in Gvat and send it to the wilderness."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:25:21.619389631Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}